  ✓ CONFIRMED: Uniswap mint callback can be triggered through an unsolicite
    Justification: Upon reviewing 
SemiFungiblePositionManager.uniswapV3MintCallback and _mintLiquidity, I 
confirmed that the callback lacks authorization that the callback originated 
from an SFPM-controlled mint operation. The callback decodes attacker-controlled
CallbackData, validates only that msg.sender is a canonical Uniswap V3 pool 
matching the supplied token and fee features, and then calls 
SafeTransferLib.safeTransferFrom(decoded.poolFeatures.token0/token1, 
decoded.payer, msg.sender, amountOwed). Because Uniswap V3 pools permit 
arbitrary callers to invoke mint with any recipient and callback data, an 
attacker can call the canonical pool directly with recipient equal to the SFPM 
and payer set to any address that has approved the SFPM for the underlying 
tokens. The callback will execute even though mintTokenizedPosition, 
_validateAndForwardToAMM, and _mintLiquidity were never entered. The per-pool 
ReentrancyLock does not protect the callback because direct pool.mint does not 
acquire it, and callback validation does not check the lock or an active 
operation context. Thus approved protocol or user funds can be pulled, subject 
to the selected liquidity and allowance.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: Owner-controlled factory can arbitrarily mint donor NFTs or 
    Justification: Upon reviewing DonorNFT.changeFactory and DonorNFT.issueNFT 
in periphery/DonorNFT.sol, I confirmed that the issuer authorization is fully 
mutable by the contract owner. The constructor initializes Owned with 
msg.sender, but does not initialize panopticFactory, and changeFactory(address 
newFactory) is externally callable by onlyOwner without rejecting address(0), 
validating the canonical PanopticFactory, emitting an event, or imposing a 
timelock or one-time restriction. issueNFT then authorizes calls solely through 
require(msg.sender == panopticFactory) and performs no validation of deployer, 
pool, token addresses, or fee before minting to the supplied deployer and 
incrementing nextId. Consequently, an owner or compromised owner account can set
panopticFactory to an attacker-controlled contract or EOA and cause arbitrary 
addresses to receive NFTs, while setting it to address(0) permanently prevents 
ordinary issuance until corrected. This is not an unauthorized external takeover
because onlyOwner remains enforced, but it is a real privileged access-control 
and operational-integrity vulnerability under the stated threat model.
